        "Eleven companies that make the surfaces of an Indian home: the tiles on the floor, the basin and taps in the bathroom, and the plywood, MDF (medium-density fibreboard, the flat board inside most modular furniture), particle board and laminates that kitchens and wardrobes are built from. This is one of five groups on the Consumer Durables page; the others cover cooling and electrical appliances, kitchen and houseware, paints and coatings, and lifestyle brands (footwear, personal care and sleep). Three make tiles: **Kajaria**, **Somany** and **Orient Bell**. Two make sanitaryware and taps: **Cera** and **Hindware Home Innovation**. Six make wood panels and laminates: **Century Plyboards**, **Greenply**, **Greenpanel**, **Rushil Decor**, **Greenlam** and **Stylam**. This edition was built from both earnings calls for every company (Hindware held no Q1 call, so its Q3 and Q4 calls and its Q1 business update were used), the FY26 annual reports, rating reports from ICRA, CRISIL, CARE and Infomerics, and sector work from ICRA, CARE Ratings and Anarock. The headline: the West Asia war shut India's tile capital, Morbi, for about six weeks from late March 2026 and doubled the gas price its kilns pay, so the branded makers raised prices 15–20% and took share; at the same time resin for wood panels rose 35–45% and Gulf exports of MDF stopped. Most Q1 FY27 growth is price, not volume, and this note judges each company on normal earnings. The annual reports turned up a ₹20cr fraud at a Kajaria bathware subsidiary, income-tax searches at Greenply, Greenlam and Greenpanel, a going-concern warning on a Hindware subsidiary, an unexplained ₹63cr jump in tax disputes at Greenlam's subsidiaries, and a change of control at Stylam, now 40% owned by Japan's Aica Kogyo. Every chart uses filed results.",
